---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import TopicList from '../../components/overview/TopicList.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../consts';

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;

const kepler = `use deep_causality_algebra::Real;
use deep_causality_core::{CausalityError, PropagatingEffect};
use deep_causality_num::{lift, lower};
use deep_causality_physics::{EARTH_GM, TwoBodyPropagator};

/// The working precision. Change this one line to \`Float106\`
/// and the whole program runs at about 31 decimal digits.
type FloatType = f64;
// (position, velocity)
type Orbit = ([FloatType; 2], [FloatType; 2]); 

fn main() {
  let epoch: Orbit = ([lift(7.0e6), lift(0.0)], [lift(0.0), lift(7.8e3)]);
  let result = PropagatingEffect::pure(epoch)
     // forward in time
     .bind_or_error(|s, _, _| kepler_step(s, lift(600.0)), "no state") 
     // next in causal order, backward in time
     .bind_or_error(|s, _, _| kepler_step(s, lift(-600.0)), "no state"); 
        
    let (p, _) = result.into_value().expect("both steps succeed");
    let dx = p[0] - lift::<FloatType>(7.0e6);
    let miss = Real::sqrt(dx * dx + p[1] * p[1]);
    println!("returned to epoch within {:.2e} m", lower(miss));
}

/// One Kepler step. The propagator is exact and accepts a negative \`dt\`.
fn kepler_step(state: Orbit, dt: FloatType) -> PropagatingEffect<Orbit> {
    let (p, v) = state;
    match TwoBodyPropagator::from_state(
        p, 
        v, 
        lift(EARTH_GM))
           .and_then(|orbit| orbit.propagate(dt))
    {
        Ok(next) => PropagatingEffect::pure(next),
        Err(e) => PropagatingEffect::from_error(CausalityError::from(e)),
    }
}`;

const keplerOut = `returned to epoch within 5.67e-10 m`;

const unifiedMathReadme = `${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/deep_causality_unified_math/README.md`;

const whatIf = `let observed = plug_in().iterate_n(30, one_minute).into_process();

let what_if = plug_in()
    .iterate_n(10, one_minute)
    // what if the cell had been at 52 °C after ten minutes?
    .alternate_value(52.0) 
    .iterate_n(20, one_minute)
    .into_process();`;

const whatIfOut = `Observed: 38.8 °C, 70.0 % charged after 30 minutes
What if : 41.2 °C, 65.8 % charged after 30 minutes
EffectLog (1 entries):
[[1790749984339484] !!ValueAlternation!!: Value(33.48417318664552) replaced with Value(52.0)`;

const readme = `${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/README.md`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Dynamic causality — DeepCausality"
  description="The causal monad composes a model on two layers: a one-directional causal order, and an unconstrained step that can hold time-symmetric physics. Three channels, an audit log, causaloids and the Causal State Machine."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Overview · Dynamic causality</p>
      <h1>A causal order with computation inside each step</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The causal monad in <code>deep_causality_core</code> composes a model on two layers. The
        outer layer decides the causal order. The inner layer, the step, computes
        anything, including physics that runs backward in time.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Two layers of composition</h2>
      <p>
        The classical formulation of causality, "if A then B, and if not A then not B," composes on
        one layer: the causal direction from A to B doubles as the direction of time, so it cannot
        express a time-symmetric relation as a cause. That rules out the fundamental equations of
        physics, from Newton's through Maxwell's to Schrödinger's, which are all time-symmetric.
      </p>
      <p>DeepCausality composes on two layers.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The outer layer is the causal order.</strong> Steps compose by monadic bind: a
          step runs after the one before it and receives its effect, state and context, and an error
          stops the chain. A causal graph arranges steps into a directed acyclic graph by
          construction, so no step can feed back into an earlier one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The inner layer is the step itself.</strong> A step, an ordinary Rust function, can
          hold a time-symmetric law of physics or any other computation. It assumes neither space nor
          time; location and time reach it through the context or as values such as the <code>dt</code> below.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The example moves a satellite 600 s forward along its orbit and then 600 s back, using the
        exact two-body propagator from <code>deep_causality_physics</code>:
      </p>
      <Code code={kepler} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <Code code={keplerOut} lang="text" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The satellite starts 7,000 km from Earth's center at 7.8 km/s, which puts it on an orbit
        between 629 km and 1,658 km altitude. The first step carries
        it 4,585 km along that orbit. The second step takes the position and velocity the first step
        produced, derives a fresh orbit from them, and runs the clock back 600 s. The satellite lands
        0.57 nanometers from where it started. At 7,000 km, neighboring <code>f64</code> values lie
        0.93 nanometers apart, so the round trip is exact to the resolution of the number type.
      </p>
      <p>
        That resolution is a parameter. Every crate in the unified mathematics stack takes its scalar
        as a type parameter, so changing the <code>FloatType</code> alias moves the whole program,
        propagator included, to another number type. With only that line changed, the round trip
        lands within about one spacing of each type, and within two at <code>BFloat16</code>:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th><code>FloatType</code></th><th>Decimal digits</th><th>Miss after the round trip</th><th>Spacing of values at 7,000 km</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>BFloat16</code></td><td>2</td><td>7.0 × 10⁴ m</td><td>3.3 × 10⁴ m</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>f32</code></td><td>7</td><td>0.25 m</td><td>0.5 m</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>f64</code></td><td>16</td><td>5.7 × 10⁻¹⁰ m</td><td>9.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Float106</code></td><td>31</td><td>2.4 × 10⁻²⁵ m</td><td>2.1 × 10⁻²⁵ m</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        At <code>Float106</code> the satellite lands 2.4 × 10⁻²⁵ m, about a quarter of a
        yoctometer, from where it started: far below anything an instrument can measure. For
        reference results, such as the values another solver must reproduce, <code>Float106</code> carries about 31 significant digits, twice as many as <code>f64</code>, for a one-line change.
        The <a href={unifiedMathReadme}>unified math README</a> explains how precision works as a parameter across the stack.
      </p>
      <p>
        The round trip is exact because Kepler's problem has a closed-form solution. Measured by the
        angle around the ellipse, the eccentric anomaly, the orbit follows simple harmonic motion, and
        one formula advances it by any time interval. The kernel solves Kepler's equation,
        which converts that angle into clock time, by Newton's method to the precision of the number
        type. No step size enters, so no truncation error accumulates, and a negative <code>dt</code> runs the same formula backward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second step follows the first in causal order: it cannot run until the first has produced
        its state, and an error in the first would stop it. In physical time it runs the other way.
        The classical formulation, with one arrow for both cause and time, cannot express that chain.
        Here bind carries the causal order, the propagator carries the physics, and the whole program
        takes 31 lines of Rust.
      </p>

      <h2>Three channels and a log</h2>
      <p>Each step receives exactly three channels from the step before it.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Channel</th><th>Type</th><th>What it gives a model</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Effect</td><td>a value, <code>None</code> for "no evidence," or a <code>RelayTo</code> command that jumps to another node of a graph</td><td>the result of the previous step</td></tr>
            <tr><td>State</td><td>any type; <code>()</code> when unused</td><td>memory carried from step to step</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Context</td><td>any type, passed as <code>Option&lt;C&gt;</code></td><td>the environment: data, time and space, with fixed or changing relations</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Without state, a step depends only on the effect it receives. With state, a step carries
        whatever history the state type holds. The context can be as simple as <code>()</code> or as
        rich as the <a href="/overview/dynamic-context/">context graph</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The log sits outside the channels: bind appends each step's entries after the earlier ones
        and never passes the log to a step, so no step can read it. No method removes or edits an
        entry, and each entry carries a wall-clock timestamp. The log records what the model computed
        and when: provenance for debugging, and evidence where an auditor asks for it.
      </p>

      <h2>Asking "what if?" in the middle of a run</h2>
      <p>
        You can replace a running chain's value, state or context. The steps after the replacement
        run on the new value, and the log records the change. The excerpt below charges a
        lithium-ion cell for 30 minutes; the charger drops to half current above 45 °C. The second
        run asks what would have happened had the cell been at 52 °C after ten minutes.
      </p>
      <Code code={whatIf} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <Code code={whatIfOut} lang="text" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        At 52 °C the charger switches to half current. The cell cools below 45 °C within five
        minutes, but the charge lost in those minutes stays lost: the second run ends 4.2 percentage
        points lower. The log names the 33.5 °C the model had computed and the 52 °C that replaced
        it. The <a href={readme}>project README</a> has the full program, and the <a href="/examples/pearl-counterfactual/">counterfactual example</a> walks Pearl's ladder of
        causation with the same method.
      </p>

      <h2>Causaloids: one step, many steps, a graph</h2>
      <p>
        A causaloid packages a causal function with an identity, in one of three forms.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Singleton.</strong> Runs one function and logs its input and output.</li>
        <li><strong>Collection.</strong> Feeds one input to every member and aggregates the results with <code>All</code>, <code>Any</code>, <code>None</code> or <code>Some(k)</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Graph.</strong> Evaluates a directed acyclic graph of causaloids in topological order. Where branches meet, it joins their effects: logical OR for booleans, the maximum for numbers. A <code>RelayTo</code> command restarts evaluation at the node it names.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <code>Causaloid::evaluate</code> runs a singleton. A collection or a graph runs through its own
        engine, <code>evaluate_collection</code> or <code>evaluate_subgraph_from_cause</code>, which a
        bind step can call. The stateful graph engine threads state along each path and returns an
        error at a join, where two states would have to merge. The <a href="/examples/aerospace-flight-envelope/">flight envelope monitor</a> uses all three
        shapes inside one stateful chain: a collection of sensor-health checks, a bind chain for state
        estimation, and a graph of envelope protections.
      </p>

      <h2>From a result to an action</h2>
      <p>
        The Causal State Machine pairs each state with an action. Each state holds a causaloid, and
        the machine fires the paired action when the causaloid's result is active. The <a href="/overview/dynamic-action/">dynamic action</a> page covers the machine.
      </p>

      <h2>What the monad leaves to you</h2>
      <p>
        Bind accepts any Rust closure as a step, so the monad cannot guarantee that a step is pure: a
        closure may read a clock, do IO or capture a variable. A causaloid narrows this by storing a
        function pointer, which cannot capture its environment. Because bind accepts any function of
        the right type, the model author decides which dependencies count as causal.
      </p>

      <h2>Machine-checked laws</h2>
      <p>
        The monad and the causaloid engine carry Lean 4 proofs: the monad laws, the structure of the
        error-and-log stack, termination of relay commands, closure of the verdict algebra, and order
        invariance of the graph join. Each theorem has one Rust test that pins the implementation to
        the same statement. The <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/lean/THEOREM_MAP.md`}>theorem
        map</a> links every proof to its test.
      </p>

      <TopicList current="dynamic-causality" />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
